<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles I of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1288">1288</a>–1342</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Charles Robert of Anjou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, also called Charles Robert, was the first Angevin king of Hungary, who won the throne after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> line died out in 1301. By 1321 he had broken the power of great oligarchs such as Matthew Csák. He reformed royal finances and introduced the gold florin, minted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kremnica">Kremnica</a> gold, and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Charles I of Hungary" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A9pes_kr%C3%B3nika_-_138.oldal_-_K%C3%A1roly_R%C3%B3bert_kir%C3%A1ly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
